The generating function for the number of ways to make \( n \) dollars using these coins is: \[
\frac{1}{(1-x^{25})(1-x^{10})(1-x^5)(1-x)}
\]

The generating function for the number of ways to make \( n \) dollars using these coins is: \[
\frac{1}{(1-x^{100})(1-x^{25})(1-x^{10})(1-x^5)}
\]

The generating function for \( P_n \) is: \[
\frac{1}{(1-x^{25})(1-x^{10})(1-x^5)(1-x)}
\]

The asymptotic behavior of the coefficient of \( x^{100n} \) is dominated by: \[
\frac{1}{1-x} \approx e^{x/(1-x)}
\]

The other factors contribute polynomial growth, so: \[
P_n \sim \frac{1}{25 \cdot 10 \cdot 5} \cdot \frac{1}{n^3} \cdot 100^n = \frac{4}{1250} \cdot \frac{100^n}{n^3}
\]

The generating function for \( B_n \) is: \[
\frac{1}{(1-x^{100})(1-x^{25})(1-x^{10})(1-x^5)}
\]

The asymptotic behavior of the coefficient of \( x^{100n} \) is dominated by: \[
\frac{1}{1-x^5} \approx e^{x^4/(1-x^5)}
\]

The other factors contribute polynomial growth, so: \[
B_n \sim \frac{1}{100 \cdot 25 \cdot 10 \cdot 5} \cdot \frac{1}{n^3} \cdot 20^n = \frac{1}{125000} \cdot \frac{20^n}{n^3}
\]
